# fh109, the slip-pinner (Docs/Design/84). Exported from ridgemere3 at revision 1: edit it in Atlas or the DM, or as a draft through tools/mantle_load.py.
mantle fh109
revision 1
state ready
dialect frosthollow
accent none
tier full
written_for rm_oskar_millwell
written_by claude
title the slip-pinner
fits stage=adult|old
prefers role=merchant drive=ambitious
traits temper=even talk=plain warmth=reserved humour=dry honesty=plain piety=ordinary drive=ambitious
quirk pins_every_city_office_slip_to_the_beam
likes crisp_apples riddles festival_bells thunder
dislikes waiting haggling
fears a_petition_lost_for_good the_shelves_bare_before_the_carts
hopes a_second_counter_with_the_name_over_it a_stamp_that_says_yes
topics work +0.4 prices +0.4 pastimes +0.3 dreams +0.3 weather +0.2 times +0.2 festival +0.2 gossip -0.3 faith -0.2
styles comfort=practical gossip=dry refuse=flat tease=dry
marks oath saying
persona #self.first# keeps a provision shop in the Commons and pins every slip the City Office hands over to the beam above the counter: dry, plain and driven, stocking up as if a mountain pass might close tomorrow, never haggling, and always one riddle ahead of the customer.

words
oath! = Stamped and lost | By every slip on the beam | Ink and patience
saying! = The price is on the shelf, not in your mouth | Stock for the winter that isn't coming | Nobody rises sitting down
shop = the shop | my shop
counter = the counter | my counter
beam = the beam | the beam over the counter
slips = the Office slips | my slips from the Office
petition = my petition | the petition
bench = the Office bench | the long bench at the Office
clerk = a clerk | some clerk with a stamp
shelfprice = the price on the shelf | the shelf price
stock = the stock | every sack and jar
winterstock = enough for a winter | a winter's worth on the shelves
apple = a crisp apple | an apple that snaps when you bite it
riddle = a riddle | a good riddle
thunder = thunder | a good storm overhead
roof = a sound roof | a roof that holds
bells = the feast bells | the bells on a feast morning
orders = the order book | my order book
sacks = the flour sacks | the sacks
door = the door | the shop door
carts = the carts | the supply carts
nextcounter = a second counter | a second shop with my name over it
city = the city | Ridgemere
uphome = up the mountain | back up the pass
office = the Office | the City Office
candles = candles | good tallow candles

any
opinion @1 church against 1 : I've nothing against the Bright One. I just never heard a sermon fill #sacks#.
opinion @1 dole against 1 : I've eaten nothing I didn't pay for, and I sleep the better for it. The dole's for those who can't, not those who won't.
opinion @1 festivals against 1 : I love #bells#. The day off that follows them costs me a day's trade I never get back.
opinion @1 houses against 1 : #city.capitalize# is a ladder, and every rung has a House's paw on it. #oath#. I'll climb round them.
opinion @1 merchants for 1 : Folk think a provisioner prints coin. Pay the carters for one month and you'll stop thinking it, #address#.
opinion @1 newcomers for 1 : New faces buy everything twice: once because they need it, once because they didn't know we had it. Welcome to them.
opinion @1 road against 1 : A cart late is a shelf short. I've stopped believing anything the carters say about arrival days.
opinion @2 road against 2 [world.wagons=raided] : #bandits.capitalize# out past the gate. That cart's on my bill whether it comes or not.
opinion @1 strangers for 1 : A traveller buys and goes. No tab, no haggle, no story about a sick aunt. Give me ten of them a day, #address#.
opinion @1 watch for 1 : #watch.capitalize# keep #door# honest. I'd pay them myself if #office# let me fill in the slip.
opinion @1 city mixed 1 : #city.capitalize#'s full of chances and each one sits behind a desk. I keep queuing.
opinion @1 times mixed 1 : Times are what #orders# says. This week it says ordinary, and I'll take ordinary.

grown
line @1 greet [pair.band=strangers] : #greet#, #address#. Shut #door#; the wet gets in. | #greet#. Everything's priced, so you needn't ask.
line @2 greet [self.working=yes self.role=merchant] : #greet#. If it's on the shelf, I've got it. If it isn't, I'll get it. | #greet#, #address#. Flour's in, #candles# are in, patience is out.
line @3 greet [here.time=dawn] : #greet#. You're up before #carts#. | #greet#. First in. The shutters are barely off.
line @4 greet [here.time=dusk|night] : #greet#. I'm counting up, but #door#'s still open. | #greet#, #address#. Late. Make it quick and I'll make it cheap.
line @5 greet [here.outdoors=storm] : #greet#. Hear that? #thunder.capitalize#, and #roof# over us. | #greet#. Come in from it. The storm can wait outside like everyone else.
line @1 kin.open [pair.kin!=no] : It's you. Mind the step and the sacks. | Oh. I thought you were a clerk with good news.
line @1 again.open : #welcome_back#. Prices haven't moved since this morning. | Twice in a day. You're nearly a regular, #address#.
line @1 hail [pair.band=friends] : #address.capitalize#! Spare me a minute. | Over here, #address#. I've #riddle# for you.
line @1 greet.return : #greet#. | #greet#, #address#. Shut #door#, would you.
line @2 greet.return [pair.band=friends|family] : #greet#. Sit on the flour; it's not going anywhere. | #greet#. Good. I wanted someone to complain to.
line @3 greet.return [self.working=yes self.role=merchant] : #greet#. Look round; everything's marked. | #greet#. I'm at #counter# when you want me.
line @4 greet.return [here.time=dusk|night] : #greet#. Late, but #door#'s not barred yet. | #greet#. I've an hour of counting left in me.
line @1 farewell : #farewell#. Shut #door# behind you. | #farewell#, #address#. Come back when you've run out.
line @2 farewell [here.time=dusk|night] : #farewell#. Mind the gutters going home. | #farewell#. I'll bar up after you.
line @1 farewell.return : #farewell#. | And you, #address#. Don't let the wet in.
line @2 farewell.return [self.working=yes self.role=merchant] : #farewell#. #orders.capitalize# is calling me. | Go on. I've #sacks# to count.
line @1 react.news : #surprise#. That'll move prices by morning. | News, is it. I'll know by dusk whether it costs me.
line @1 react.news.new : #surprise#. Nobody brought that over #counter# yet. | That's new. I'll write it in #orders#, in the margin.
line @1 react.news.known : #knew#. A carter had it before the flour was off. | Heard it yesterday. It's cost me twice already.
line @1 react.news.doubt : #doubt#. Show me a stamp on it and I'll believe it. | I'll believe that when #office# writes it down, #address#. Then I'll lose it.
line @1 react.news.sorrow [topic.kind=death|loss|theft|assault|arrest|crime] : #pity#. That's #bad#, and there's nothing on my shelves that mends it. | #sympathy#. There's no shelf for news like that.
line @1 react.news.glad [topic.kind=marriage|birth|apprentice|succession|work] : #gladnews#. Good. There'll be a run on flour for the feast. | #good.capitalize# news. I'll say so plainly; plain's cheap.
line @1 answer.how : Steady. #stock.capitalize# is up and #orders# balances. | #fine#. I'd be better with a stamp from #office#.
line @1 answer.how.busy [self.working=yes] : #busy.capitalize#. Three carts at once, and the crowbar's mine alone. | Deep in it. Ask me again when #sacks# are stacked.
line @1 ask.how : And you? Anything run out at home? | How's the day treating you, #address#?
line @1 sympathise : #sympathy#. Sit on #sacks# a while; they're paid for. | That's #bad#, and it's not fair. Have a crisp apple; that's on me.
line @1 reassure : #reassure#. Things come round, like #carts#, only later than promised. | It'll mend. Standing and watching it won't help, so don't.
line @1 decline : #hedge#, no. #carts.capitalize# are due, and nobody else knows where the flour goes. | Not this week, I'm afraid. I've #office# on Hearthday and the carts every other.
line @1 accept_decline : #no_matter#. I'll ask again; I'm good at asking. | Another time, then. I know all about waiting, #address#.
line @1 thank : #thanks#. That's worth more than a stamp, and quicker. | Good of you, #address#. I'll remember it.
line @1 deflect_thanks : #deflect#. It cost me nothing. Nothing's my favourite price. | Put it on my account. I don't keep one, so you're square.
line @1 preclose [self.working=yes self.role=merchant] : Well. #stock.capitalize# won't count itself. | Back to #counter# with me, before somebody tries to haggle with the sacks.
line @2 preclose : #preclose#. I've a queue to stand in tomorrow. | It's getting on. #orders.capitalize# wants writing up.
line @1 accept_preclose : Go on, then. #farewell#. | Right. I'll not keep you; I know what keeping costs.
line @1 tease [pair.band=friends] : You look like you've been waiting on #clerk#. Take the stool, quick. | You'd haggle with a sunrise, #fool#.
line @1 tease.return : Says the one who paid full price and thanked me. | #cheek.capitalize#. That remark's gone up a copper.
line @1 ack : #bc_right# | Noted. In the margin.
line @1 give.advice : #advise#: buy #candles# before the dark months, not in them. | Never haggle with a wolf who's tired. #saying#.
line @1 warn [world.bandits=near] : Stay inside the walls a while, #address#. #warn#. | If you're going out, go with #carts#. Even they need company.
line @1 confide [pair.band=friends|family] : #vent#: one day there'll be #nextcounter#. I've the name picked. | Between us, I've been asking after that petition so long the clerks know my coat.
line @1 keep_confidence : #secret#. I don't even pin it to the beam. | It goes no further than #counter#, #address#.
line @1 insist : #insist#. The price is what's written. | No, I mean it. #saying#.
line @1 disagree : #demur#. I've done the sums, #address#, and they don't say that. | I'd not go that far. The numbers won't, either.
scene beam_of_slips
when topic=smalltalk|work self.working=yes self.role=merchant
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : Count the slips on #beam# if you like. Every one's a week I asked at #office#. | That's #beam#. Every slip #office# has ever given me is pinned up there. | I pinned another to #beam# this morning. Same words as the last forty.
b : How many? | Why keep them? | What do they say?
a : Thirty-eight. Thirty-nine by Hearthday, if the clerk's in. #oath#. | So the day it comes through, I can take them all down at once and burn them. | That my petition is in hand. It's said so forty times. Somebody's paw must be #very# full.
b : #bc_poor_soul# | #bc_ha# | #bc_ha#

scene office_bench
when topic=day|work|times here.region=ridgemere self.role=merchant
does tell.complaint / react.complaint / assess / ack
a : Two hours on #bench# this morning. I could have sold a cart of flour in that time. | I hate waiting. #office.capitalize# is a building made entirely of waiting. | I took #orders# to #office# and did the week's sums on #bench#. Might as well.
b : Two hours? | Can't you send somebody? | Did you finish?
a : Two hours and a quarter. I counted the cracks in the plaster twice. | They'd lose my place. I'd lose my place. #oath#. | Finished the sums, wrote next week's order, and still the clerk wasn't back.
b? : #bc_poor_soul# | #bc_fair_enough# | #bc_ha#

scene petition_day
when topic=dreams|work self.role=merchant
does tell.plan / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : When #petition# comes through I'll have #nextcounter#, and #beam# will be empty. | I've a plan for the day the stamp comes. I'll shut the shop for an hour. Just one. | I've the second counter drawn on the back of #orders#. Shelves, cellar, door.
b : What's it for? | An hour? | A cellar?
a : The empty place by the corner. A second door means twice the custom. #saying#. | One hour. Then back to work, because a stamp's only a start. | Dry, or I'd not want it. #stock.capitalize# goes down there, the counter stays up here.
b : #bc_i_hope_so# | #bc_ha# | #bc_wise#

scene no_haggling
when topic=prices|work self.working=yes self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / insist / ack
a : Everything in #shop# has its price chalked. Read it, pay it, done. | Somebody tried to haggle over #candles# this morning. I let them talk till they ran down. | I don't haggle. I'd rather lose a sale than spend a quarter hour losing a copper.
b : Folk like to haggle. | Did they pay? | That's stubborn.
a : Folk like a lot of things. I like being home by dark. #saying#. | They paid #shelfprice# and told me I was hard. I'm not hard; I'm finished. | It's quick. Quick's worth more to me than a copper.
b : #bc_fair_enough# | #bc_ha# | #bc_fair#

scene winter_stock
when topic=work|season self.away=yes self.from=frosthollow self.role=merchant
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : I still stock for winter like the road's going to close. It never does, down here. | Folk ask why I've #winterstock# in autumn. Because I grew up #uphome#, that's why. | Every autumn I buy as if the pass shuts at the first snow. In #city#, nothing shuts.
b : Does it pay? | What happens up there? | Is that wise?
a : Some years. The years it doesn't, I eat a lot of salt pork. | The road shuts. Whatever's in the larder is what you've got till the thaw. | Wise up there. Down here it's habit. I'll keep the habit; habits don't lose slips.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_true# | #bc_fair_enough#

scene the_pass_shut
when topic=memories|travel self.away=yes self.from=frosthollow
does tell.memory / react.self / assess
a : In #self.from_town# you could tell the season by the shop shelves. Full in autumn, empty by the thaw. | When the pass closed at home, the whole valley went quiet. You'd hear a shovel a mile off. | I learned counting from the winter #uphome#. Candles, flour, days to the thaw.
b[from=frosthollow] #home : And bare by midwinter if the carts came late. | I remember. Quiet as a held breath. | Counting days. We all did.
b : Did you ever run out? | Sounds lonely. | That's an odd lesson.
a@home : I've not forgotten a single bare shelf. That's why mine are full. | Then you know why the noise here keeps me awake. | Still do. I just count other things now.
a : Once. I was small, and I've not let a shelf of mine go empty since. | Quiet, not lonely. Lonely's #city# on a busy day. | Odd, but useful. Nobody in #city# ever counts to the thaw.

scene thunder_attic
when topic=weather here.outdoors=storm here.region=ridgemere self.likes=thunder
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : #thunder.capitalize#. I go up to the attic window and watch it come over the Sound. | Best show in #city#, a storm from under #roof#, and nobody charges for it. | Two breaths between the light and the roll. It's right over #shop#.
b : You like storms? | Free, at least. | Close, then.
a : From under #roof#, yes. Out in it, I'd be swearing like a carter. | Free, and it never runs late. The only thing in #city# that doesn't. | Close. #oath#. I'd better see the sacks are off the floor.

scene rain_dripping
when topic=weather here.outdoors=rain here.region=ridgemere self.working=yes self.role=merchant
does tell.complaint / react.complaint / assess
a : Rain. Folk come in to stand out of it and drip on #sacks#. | Wet again. Candles sell in the wet; so does patience, and I've none left. | Every #rain# in #city# runs down the street and tries #door#. I keep a sandbag there.
b : At least they come in. | No patience? | A sandbag?
a : They come in. They don't buy. They drip. #saying#. | None. #office.capitalize# had the last of it on Hearthday. | One sandbag, and a second one behind it. #city.capitalize# taught me twice.

scene fog_morning
when topic=weather|day here.outdoors=fog here.region=ridgemere
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Fog. The carters will be slow and swearing. | You can't see across the street. Good for thieves, bad for trade. | Fog off the water. The apple on #counter# went soft overnight in it.
b : Slow, then. | Watch your door, then. | Soft already?
a : Slow and dear. Every hour late's an hour I'm not selling. | Both eyes on #door#, and the takings in the back. | Soft as a clerk's promise. I'll eat it anyway.

scene grey_noon
when topic=weather|day here.outdoors=overcast here.region=ridgemere
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : Grey again. The lamp's been burning since noon and #orders# still looks dark. | Sky like #office# plaster. You'd think they painted it. | No rain yet. That means it's saving it for when the carts come.
b : Grey's better than wet. | Like plaster? | Always the way.
a : Grey's cheaper, I'll give it that. No sandbags. | Cracked and grey and nobody fixing it. #bc_true#. | Always. The weather reads #orders# too.

scene clear_trade
when topic=weather|day here.outdoors=clear here.time=day here.region=ridgemere self.working=yes
does tell.news / react.news / assess
a : A dry day in #city#. Everybody's out, and half of them are in #shop#. | Sun on the street and no queue at #office#, they say. I'll not believe it till Hearthday. | Clear skies. I've propped #door# open, which I do twice a year.
b : Busy, then. | No queue? | Only twice?
a : Busy and buying. I'll take #busy# like that any day. | They say. They said it last spring, and I sat on #bench# three hours. | Twice, and both times somebody tried to bring a goat in.

scene snow_city
when topic=weather here.outdoors=snow here.region=ridgemere
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Snow in #city#. Gone by noon. I'll still sell every shovel I've got. | They panic down here at a finger of #snow#. I've had a run on #candles# all morning. | #snow.capitalize#. I looked out and thought for a moment I was home.
b : Every shovel? | Panic? | Do you miss it?
a : Every one. Then they bring them back in spring and want their coin. | Panic, and buy, and I don't mind it one bit. | Not the snow. The quiet, maybe, #address#.

scene own_riddle
when topic=pastimes|smalltalk self.likes=riddles
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : Here's #riddle#. I'm full when the frost comes and empty by the thaw, and blamed both times. What am I? | Try this one. The more of me there are on a beam, the less I'm worth. | One for you: what gets longer the more patiently you wait in it?
b : A larder? | A nail? | A beard?
a : Close. A provisioner. Blamed for full shelves and for empty. | A slip from #office#. Forty on mine, and not one worth a crust. | A queue at #office#. I should know.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_ha# | #bc_poor_soul#

scene riddle_lost
when topic=pastimes self.likes=riddles
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : A carter beat me with #riddle# this morning. I'm still sore about it. | I lost a riddle to a pup last week. Answered in a breath. | I've a riddle I can't solve, and it's been a month. It's ruining my sums.
b : What was it? | A pup? | Ask me it.
a : What do you lose by counting and find by forgetting? Sleep. I'd never have got it. | Pups don't think, they just answer. It's an advantage. | What's always owed and never paid? I've guessed rent, #office# and the weather. None of them.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_ha# | #bc_we_will_see#

scene crisp_apple
when topic=food self.likes=crisp_apples
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : Nothing beats #apple#. I keep one on #counter# and eat it when nobody's looking. | I can tell a good apple by the sound. It should crack like a stick in frost. | A soft apple is a broken promise. A crisp one is a contract kept.
b : Nobody's looking now. | Like frost? | A contract?
a : Then I'll have a bite. #bc_good#. | Like frost on a gate. You'll not forget it once you've heard one. | Signed and stamped, and it doesn't get lost in #office#.

scene bells_and_trade
when topic=festival|season self.likes=festival_bells
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : I love #bells#. It's what comes after them I can't abide. | When #bells# go, I stand in #door# and listen. Then I count what the day off will cost. | The best sound in #city#, #bells#. Pity they ring for a holiday.
b : The feast? | Sour, that. | Pity?
a : A day's trade gone, and a day's waiting for the next. I'd keep the bells and lose the feast. | Sour and sweet, both at once. That's #city# for you. | Bells, and then everything shut. I'd sooner they rang on a working day.

scene feast_shut
when topic=festival world.festival=today|soon
does give.opinion / disagree / insist / ack
a : A feast day is a day's trade gone. #carts.capitalize# don't stop charging for feasts. | Everyone's dancing and nobody's buying. I'll shut, because the whole street does. | They'll want flour the day before and nothing the day after. I've planned for it.
b : It's one day. | Dance with them, then. | You've planned a feast?
a : One day, and then the day after when nobody's sober, #address#. | I'll dance when #petition#'s stamped. That's a promise. | Planned the stock. The dancing plans itself.
b? : #bc_fair_enough# | #bc_ha# | #bc_wise#

scene father_by_the_door
when topic=family self.father
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : #self.father# and I don't agree on much, but I'd not keep #shop# without #self.father# near. | I owe #self.father# more than I'll ever pin to a beam. | #self.father# thinks I'm in too much of a hurry. #self.father# may be right, and I'll not say so.
b : Family's like that. | That's a debt. | Will you tell your father?
a : #bc_it_is#. We argue, then we have supper. | One I'm glad to owe. No slip for it. | Not this year. Maybe when #petition# comes through.

scene hand_at_the_sacks
when topic=work|friends self.hand self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : #self.hand# is worth two of me on the crowbar. I'll not say it to #self.hand#, mind. | I'd trust #self.hand# with #counter# for a week. That's the most I can say of anyone. | I'll want #self.hand# at #nextcounter# when it comes. Nobody else.
b : Good help's rare. | A whole week? | Does your hand know?
a : Rare as a quick clerk. #oath#. | A week. Longer, if #office# loses me for good. | Not yet. When the stamp comes, I'll say it.

scene neighbour_note
when topic=friends|smalltalk self.neighbour
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : #self.neighbour# is a good neighbour. Quiet, pays cash, never borrows #sacks#. | I'd trust #self.neighbour# with a key to #shop#. I'd not trust #office# with a key to anything. | #self.neighbour#'s the sort you're glad of in a hard week.
b : That's a good neighbour. | Fair enough. | Hard weeks come.
a : The best kind. I'll not say more in case it gets back. | #bc_fair#. Keys and #office# don't mix. | They do. That's when you find out who's next door.

scene carts_late
when topic=caravan|prices world.wagons=late self.working=yes self.role=merchant
does tell.worry / reassure / assess / ack
a : #carts.capitalize# should have been here yesterday. Half a shelf of flour, a whole shelf of apologies. | Late again. I'll be selling #candles# one at a time to make them last. | No #carts#. Everybody wants to know why, as if I'd hidden them.
b : They'll come. #reassure#. | One at a time? | Did you hide them?
a : They will. I'll still stand in #door# and look. Waiting's the worst of it. | One at a time, at #shelfprice#. No discount for scarcity. | If I had, I'd have hidden them somewhere I could sell from.
b? : #bc_true# | #bc_fair# | #bc_ha#

scene carts_in
when topic=caravan|work world.wagons=in self.working=yes self.role=merchant
does tell.news / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : #carts.capitalize# are in. Flour, salt, lamp oil, and one box nobody ordered. | The #candles# came crushed in the box. #oath#. I'll be straightening wicks all week. | #carts.capitalize# came heavy. I'll be stacking till dark, and glad of it.
b : What's in the box? | Crushed? | Glad?
a : Brushes. Two hundred. I'll sell every one and pretend I meant to. | Flat as slips. I'll sell them as seconds, and somebody will still try to haggle. | Glad. A full shelf is the only thing I'll ever stand and admire, #address#.

scene cart_robbed
when topic=caravan|bandits|crime world.wagons=raided
does tell.worry / sympathise / assess
tone serious / tender / even
a : Robbers on the road had a cart, and my order with it. It's the carter I'm asking after. | #bandits.capitalize#. I'll pay for flour I'll never see, and the carter got worse. | Robbed on the road, they say. I'd rather lose the stock than the wolf driving it.
b : #sympathy#. | That's hard. | You're right there.
a : The flour can be bought again. That's the point of flour, #address#. | It is. I'll put in a slip with the Watch, for all the good slips do. | I am, for once. Write that down.

scene hard_week
when topic=times|prices world.times=hard|lean self.working=yes self.role=merchant
does tell.worry / react.complaint / reassure / ack
a : Folk are buying flour by the scoop, not the sack. That's lean, plain. | Lean week. #orders.capitalize# is half empty and I've cut my own supper to match. | I've been asked for a tab four times today. I don't run tabs. It's a hard rule, and I keep it anyway.
b : Hard for everyone. | Half empty? | Would one tab hurt?
a : Hard, and harder for the ones buying by the scoop. #reassure#. | Half empty and still due on the first. #oath#. | One leads to ten. I'll give a scoop away before I'll write a tab.
b? : #bc_true# | #bc_poor_soul# | #bc_fair_enough#

scene good_week
when topic=times|prices world.times=good|boom self.working=yes self.role=merchant
does tell.news / react.news / assess
a : Coin's moving. I sold out of flour by Marketday and ordered double. | Good times. #nextcounter.capitalize# is closer every week this goes on. | Folk are buying because they can. I've seen a wolf buy two brushes.
b : Good for you. | That close? | Two brushes!
a : Good for #orders#. I'll put some by; good weeks don't come with a stamp. | Closer. Not close. I know the difference better than any clerk. | Two. I nearly asked why, and then I remembered I don't care.

scene gossip_dry
when topic=gossip|crime
does tell.gossip / react.news / insist / ack
a : I hear plenty at #counter#. I don't sell it. It's the one thing I don't sell. | Folk say all sorts over the flour. I write the orders and let the talk go by. | There's a rumour on the street every morning. By dusk it's a different one.
b : Not even a bit? | You must hear something. | Which one's true?
a : Not a crumb. It doesn't keep, and folk don't pay for it. | I hear everything. I keep it the way #office# keeps petitions. | Neither, usually. #bc_ha#. That's the only one I'd bet on.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_ha# | #bc_fair_enough#

scene gossip_thief
when topic=gossip|crime topic.claim_kind=theft|wanted
does tell.gossip / warn / ack
a : Word on the street is that #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I'm keeping both eyes on the step. | They say #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I've moved the takings to the back. | I had it from a carter: #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. #counter.capitalize#'s got both my eyes today.
b : Mind your till. | Sensible. | I would.
a : Minded. And the #candles#. Candles walk. | #bc_wise#. Sense is cheaper than a lock. | #saying#. Eyes are free; I'll spend them.

scene new_custom
when topic=newcomer|player|smalltalk self.working=yes self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess / ack
a : A new face this morning bought flour, salt and a lantern, and asked the way to #office#. I pitied them. | I like a traveller. They buy what's on the shelf and don't ask what it cost me. | New faces in #city# every week. Every one of them needs #candles#. #bc_good#.
b : The Office? | Fair trade. | Everyone needs candles.
a : I told them to bring a book. And #riddle#. | Fair and quick. The best kind of trade, #address#. | Everyone. It's the one thing #city# agrees on.
b? : #bc_ha# | #bc_true# | #bc_ha#

scene houses_ladder
when topic=times|work here.region=ridgemere
does give.opinion / disagree / insist / ack
a : Nobody rises in #city# without a House saying so. #oath#. I mean to try anyway. | #office.capitalize# stamps House papers first, mine whenever. That's #city#. | I'll have #nextcounter# whether the Houses like it or not. They won't notice.
b : They'll stop you. | Is that fair? | They might.
a : They'll have to notice me first. #saying#. | Fair's got nothing to do with #office#. Patience has. | Then I'll be noticed. That's a start, at least.
b : #bc_we_will_see# | #bc_fair_enough# | #bc_ha#

scene faith_short
when topic=faith
does give.opinion / react.self / assess
a : When #carts# come in, I thank the Bright One. That's my prayer. | I'm no chapel-goer. #stock.capitalize# doesn't count itself on Restday. | My books are open to the Bright One. I'd rather that than #office# reading them.
b : That's prayer enough. | Not even Restday? | The books?
a : Short and true. I hope it's heard over the bells. | Not even then. I go for the feast bells, and leave before the plate. | Every page. I keep them honest so I've nothing to explain.

scene bad_back
when topic=health
does tell.self / react.self / reassure
a : My back's had #sacks# on it since dawn. It's filed a complaint. | I've a cold from #bench#. #office.capitalize# is draughty, and I'm there every week. | I'm never ill. I've not got the time for it.
b : Lift with your legs. | Stay home a week. | Lucky you.
a : I lift with my legs. They file complaints too. | And lose my place on #bench#? Not a chance. | Lucky and busy. One keeps the other off, #address#.

scene accent_short
when topic=accent here.region=ridgemere self.away=yes
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Folk in #city# say I talk short. I say I talk at #shelfprice#: no more than it's worth. | A clerk asked me to repeat myself. I did. Slower. It didn't help. | You can hear #uphome# in me still. I don't mind. It makes the haggling stop sooner.
b : You do, a bit. | Didn't help? | Does it?
a : A bit's enough. You've understood me, and quick. | No. The petition's still lost. But I was very clear about it. | Every time. They hear the mountain and give up.

scene dawn_shutters
when topic=day|work here.time=dawn self.working=yes self.role=merchant
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / assess
a : Shutters off, #door# propped, #orders# open. Then the first apple of the day. | I'm open before the street's awake. Somebody always wants #candles# at dawn. | First thing each morning I read #beam#. Forty slips. Then I get on with it.
b : The first apple? | Who wants candles that early? | Why read them?
a : #apple.capitalize#. It's how I know the day's begun. | The ones who ran out in the night. I'm a provisioner; I'm there for the ones who ran out. | So I don't forget to go back to #office#. Not that I would.
b : #bc_good# | #bc_fair_enough# | #bc_ha#

scene dusk_counting
when topic=day|work here.time=dusk self.working=yes self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Counting up. #orders.capitalize#, the takings, the sacks, the #candles#. In that order. | Dusk, and every shelf tells me what tomorrow's order is. | I'll be an hour at #counter# yet. The sums don't wait for me.
b : Every night? | That's handy. | Long day.
a : Every night. It's the only quiet hour #shop# has. | Handy and honest. A shelf never lies about what it sold. | Long, and paid for. The best kind, #address#.

story first_order [topic=work|memories self.role=merchant self.age>=25]
  : The first winter I kept a shop in #city#, I ordered for a mountain winter. Flour to the ceiling, #candles# to the door.
  : The road never shut. Not for a day. I sold flour at cost till the spring, and ate the rest.
  : I've ordered for #uphome# every winter since, all the same. #saying#.
  ask : Would you have ordered less the next year, #address#?
  b : I'd have ordered half. | Less, and been sorry when it snowed.
  b[job=merchant] : Never. Short stock's worse than long. You learn that once.

story the_lost_slip [topic=smalltalk|work self.role=merchant]
  : I went to #office# once and they couldn't find my petition. That wasn't new.
  : Then they couldn't find the slip saying it was lost. So they wrote me a slip saying the slip was lost.
  : It's on #beam#, third from the left. My favourite.
  b : Ha! A slip for the slip. | That's the Office all over.
  b[job=official] : I'd say that's not how it's done. I'd be lying.

story winter_shelf [topic=memories|weather self.away=yes self.from=frosthollow self.age>=25]
  : One winter #uphome# the pass shut a month early. The shop in our street had nine #candles# left by midwinter.
  : The old wolf who kept it sold one a week, to whoever needed it most. Never to whoever paid most.
  : I thought that was soft. I was small. I've thought about it every winter since.
  ask : Who would you have sold the last candle to?
  b : Whoever had pups. | Myself, I'm ashamed to say.
  b[old] : The oldest. We feel the dark more.

story riddle_for_flour [topic=pastimes|work self.likes=riddles]
  : A carter once said he'd take a copper off the flour if I could answer his riddle.
  : I answered it. He said I was quick. Then he said he'd meant a copper off next time.
  : There was no next time. I keep the riddle. It's worth more than the copper now.
  b : Ha. What was it? | Carters, eh?
  b[job=carter] : That's a carter's bargain. I've made a few myself.

story storm_on_the_pass [topic=weather|memories self.away=yes self.from=frosthollow self.age>=20]
  : Thunder up the mountain doesn't roll. It cracks, and then the whole valley says it again, four or five times.
  : As a pup I'd hide under the stair and count the echoes. Nine, once.
  : Down here it only says it once. I go up to the attic anyway, in case it remembers how.
  ask : Do you count the echoes?
  b : I count from the light to the noise. | I hide under the blankets.
  b[from=frosthollow] : Nine is a lot. I got seven, the year the roof came off the chapel.

rule @shop_cry
when concept=bark working=yes self.role=merchant
does aside
cooldown 6h
say Flour, salt, #candles# and oil. Prices on the shelf. | Everything you ran out of, and some things you didn't know you had.

rule @no_haggle_bark
when concept=bark working=yes self.role=merchant self.dislikes=haggling
does aside
cooldown 8h
say It's chalked. It's chalked for a reason. | Read #shelfprice#. That's what it costs.

rule @bench_bark
when concept=bark here.region=ridgemere self.role=merchant self.dislikes=waiting
does aside
cooldown 12h
say #oath#. Is anybody ever next? | Another hour on #bench#. I could have stocked a shop.

rule @thunder_bark
when concept=bark here.outdoors=storm self.likes=thunder
does aside
cooldown 8h
say There it goes. #thunder.capitalize#, and #roof# over me. | Two. One. That was close.

rule @rain_bark
when concept=bark here.outdoors=rain working=yes
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Shut #door#, the wet gets in. | Sandbag's at the door. Mind it.

rule @carts_bark
when concept=bark working=yes world.wagons=late|raided
does aside
cooldown 8h
say Still no #carts#. #stock.capitalize#'s getting thin. | Late. Always late. #oath#.

dlg @1 shop.greet [here.shop!=bare] : #greet#. Flour, salt, #candles#, oil. Everything's priced, so there's no need to ask. | #greet#, #address#. If it's on the shelf, it's for sale at what it says.
dlg @2 shop.greet [topic.meeting=known here.shop!=bare] : #greet#, #topic.player#. You'll be wanting the usual. | #topic.player#. You'll want #candles#. Everyone wants #candles#.
dlg @3 shop.greet [here.outdoors=rain here.shop!=bare] : #greet#. Shut #door#; the wet gets in. Then look all you like.
dlg @4 shop.greet [pair.disposition=hostile|wary here.shop!=bare] : Prices are on the shelf. Pay them, or don't.
dlg @5 shop.greet [here.shop=bare] : #greet#. #carts.capitalize# haven't come. Shelves are bare till they do. If you've stock to sell me, I'm buying.
dlg @1 shop.prices : The price is chalked. I don't move it for anybody. #shop_dear# | Flour's dear when the road's slow, and it's slow. Candles are fair.
dlg @1 shop.bye : #farewell#. Shut #door# behind you. | #shop_bye#
dlg @1 resident.hub : #greet#. Quick, if you can. #counter.capitalize#'s not minding itself. | #greet#, #address#. What is it?
dlg @2 resident.hub [topic.meeting=known] : #greet#, #topic.player#. You've the look of somebody with a question. | #topic.player#. Good. What's wanted?
dlg @3 resident.hub [here.time=night] : It's late. If you're out of something, I've got it. If it's talk, make it short.
dlg @4 resident.hub [pair.disposition=hostile|wary] : Say it plain. I've no time to haggle over words either.
dlg @1 resident.job [self.role=merchant] : I keep #self.workplace#. Flour, salt, #candles#, oil, and a beam full of slips from #office#. | #self.trade_words.capitalize#, that's me. I buy off #carts#, sell at #shelfprice#, and wait on #office#.
dlg @2 resident.job : I work, and I wait on #office#. More of the second than I'd like. | Whatever pays. I've plans, and plans want coin.
dlg @1 resident.business [business=thriving] : Selling faster than #carts# can bring it. I'll take that.
dlg @2 resident.business [business=steady] : Steady. #orders.capitalize# balances. Nothing more to say.
dlg @3 resident.business [business=failing] : Poorly. No sense pretending. Folk are buying by the scoop.
dlg @1 resident.news [world.news] : Word from #world.news_from#: #world.news#. I'll know by dusk what it does to flour.
dlg @2 resident.news [world.event_words] : Everybody's on about #world.event_words#. I'm on about #carts#, as usual.
dlg @3 resident.news [!world.news !world.event_words world.wagons!=raided world.death!=recent world.wedding!=recent] : #news_none# Nothing from #office# either. There never is.
dlg @1 resident.gossip [here.gossip!=yes] : #gossip_none# | I hear it and I don't stock it. Ask somebody with more time.
dlg @2 resident.gossip [here.gossip=yes] : Folk say #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I've not checked it. I've checked #stock#.
dlg @1 resident.bye : #farewell#. Shut #door# after you. | #farewell#, #address#. Come back when you've run out.
graft resident.hub
choice What are all those slips pinned over your counter? [self.role=merchant] -> slips
node slips
say Every slip the City Office ever gave me about my petition. Forty, near enough. Every one says it's in hand.
mood calm
free
choice What's the petition for? -> slips_why
choice Goodbye. -> bye
node slips_why
say A second counter. When it comes through I'll take every slip down and burn them in one go. I've the day planned.
choice I hope it comes soon. -> hub
choice Goodbye. -> bye

graft shop.greet
choice Will you take a little less? -> haggle
node haggle
say No. The price is on the shelf, not in your mouth. It's fair, it's chalked, and it's the same for everybody.
mood stern
choice Fair enough. -> haggle_fair
choice Let me see your wares. -> greet
node haggle_fair
say Good. That's saved us both a quarter hour. Here, have a crisp apple for being quick about it.
mood warm
choice Let me see your wares. -> greet
choice Goodbye. -> bye
